  "slug": "quantum-error-correction-4176",
  "summary": "Eight November 2026 preprints tie measured noise structure, including correlated leakage, to calibration, code geometry, simulation and validation on superconducting devices.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is treating non-ideal hardware noise in error correction and mitigation as structured, measurable and exploitable. A 105-qubit superconducting experiment scales surface-code stability tests to 7x7 patches and identifies temporally correlated heating-to-|3> leakage. Magic state cultivation with lattice-surgery escapes is implemented on IBM hardware. GEARS uses gate-error amplification with syndrome feedback to recalibrate during QEC, cutting measurement needs about eightfold. Logical error prediction under biased circuit-level noise guides rectangular surface-code geometry selection and reduces qubit overhead. Ellis simulates qubit loss and leakage with a Pauli-frame method. Complementary near-term work extends bnZNE mitigation to randomized circuits and proposes a self-baseline testing oracle with a controlled false-positive rate. The common novelty is linking characterized noise to calibration, resource choices and validation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
